From 00a88a1b2f6253529fc900c45aa98b1338b7b1d4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Patch <patch@local>
Date: Sat, 22 Aug 2026 17:40:25 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Add per-user favorites, filtered from the avatar chip row

Everything the app records about a photo is currently communal -- a
reaction is a tally everyone reads, a comment is addressed to the group,
the pin and the timestamp are explicitly the trip's rather than the
uploader's. There was no way to say "I want to find this one again"
without saying it to five other people.

Favorites are the private half of that. A row in `favorites` is keyed on
(photo_id, user_id), is only ever read back scoped to the caller, and no
count or authorship is exposed to anyone else. That privacy is what
makes the feature usable: you can star your own photo, or the
unflattering one you mean to come back to and delete, without it landing
as a public verdict the way a heart does.

  - Schema: `favorites`, cascading from both photos and users, with
    `favorites_user_idx` on (user_id, created_at). Every read is "mine",
    which the (photo_id, user_id) primary key can't serve.
  - PUT /api/photos/[id]/favorite takes the state it wants rather than a
    toggle, so a double tap on a slow connection can't settle on the
    opposite answer from the one on screen. It reads the photo row first
    so a star on something just deleted answers 404 instead of tripping
    the foreign key into a 500.
  - GET /api/photos loads the caller's whole starred set in one query
    rather than filtering by the page's ids: one person's shortlist is
    small, and it keeps `isFavorite` free of any join fan-out.
  - The star sits in the lightbox header, `f` on the keyboard, optimistic
    with a rollback to the object the server still believes in. Both
    lightbox call sites already route changes through `onPhotoChange`,
    so the map gets it too. The grid marks starred photos in the same
    corner cluster as reactions and comments.

Browse's `who: string | null` becomes a `View` union, so "Favorites" and
a person can't both be lit -- that combination would read as "Bryan's
favorites", which isn't a thing this app has. The chip renders at zero
on purpose: it's the only place the feature announces itself, and the
empty state is where the star gets explained.

Needs `npm run db:push` (or `drizzle-kit generate` if you'd rather keep
the migration in the repo). By hand:

  CREATE TABLE favorites (
    photo_id   text NOT NULL REFERENCES photos(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
    user_id    text NOT NULL REFERENCES users(id)  ON DELETE CASCADE,
    created_at timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),
    PRIMARY KEY (photo_id, user_id)
  );
  CREATE INDEX favorites_user_idx ON favorites (user_id, created_at);
